<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bergpark Wilhelmshöhe</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Wilhelmshöhe</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bergpark Wilhelmshöhe is a large landscape park on a hillside west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassel">Kassel</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, laid out by the landgraves of Hesse-Kassel from the late 17th century. It contains the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hercules-kassel">Hercules monument</a>, water cascades and fountains, and Wilhelmshöhe Palace, built from 1786 for Landgrave William IX. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> was held at the palace after his capture at Sedan in 1870, and the park became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2013.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bergpark-wilhelmshohe.jpg" alt="Bergpark Wilhelmshöhe" width="318" height="458" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hendrik Thole · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Herkules_Monument,_Bergpark_Wilhelmsh%C3%B6he,_Kassel_2004.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bad Wilhelmshöhe is a district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassel">Kassel</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>, west of the city centre at the foot of the Habichtswald hills. The park covers about 2.4 square kilometres and rises from Kassel to the Karlsberg at 526.2 metres, which makes it the largest European hillside park. At its summit stands the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hercules-kassel">Hercules monument</a>, a 40-metre pyramid topped by an 8.5-metre <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> statue of Hercules. Water from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reservoir">reservoirs</a> behind it runs down through a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> water <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, a grotto, fountains and waterfalls, including a Great Cascade 350 metres long, to a pond with the Great Fountain, which at about 50 metres was the largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in 1767.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Canons Regular from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> founded the Weißenstein monastery on the site of the palace in 1143, and after the Reformation Landgrave Philip I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a> used its buildings as a hunting lodge. The park began as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> garden under Landgrave Charles I of Hesse-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassel">Kassel</a>, and in 1701 the Italian architect Giovanni Francesco Guerniero started the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hercules-kassel">Hercules monument</a> and the giant cascades. In 1785 Landgrave William IX began a large extension, and the next year Simon Louis du Ry designed the Neoclassical palace. Garden taste shifted to the English style, and Heinrich Christoph Jussow added artificial ruins such as the Löwenburg. The Great Waterfall, ordered in 1826 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ii-elector-of-hesse">William II, Elector of Hesse</a>, was the last large work.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassel">Kassel</a> became the capital of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a> in 1806, and its king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerome-bonaparte">Jérôme Bonaparte</a> held court at the palace, renamed Napoleonshöhe, until 1813. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> annexed Hesse-Kassel in 1866, ending the dynasty that had made the park. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> was held at the palace after the Battle of Sedan in 1870. From 1899 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-ii">Wilhelm II</a> used it as a summer residence, and from November 1918 to February 1919 the Oberste Heeresleitung under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-von-hindenburg">Paul von Hindenburg</a> directed the withdrawal of German troops from here.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Allied bombs damaged the palace in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, and from 1968 to 1974 it was rebuilt as an art museum with Old Master paintings, Graeco-Roman antiques and a wallpaper collection. In 1972 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/willy-brandt">Willy Brandt</a> and Willi Stoph met there for talks between the two German states. The State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a> administers the park, which belongs to the Museumslandschaft Hessen Kassel. It was proclaimed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> on 23 June 2013 for its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> architecture and unusual water features. Georg Dehio called it possibly the most grandiose combination of landscape and architecture that the Baroque had dared.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bergpark Wilhelmshöhe” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Kassel, the capital, retained the <b>Wilhelmshöhe</b> palace park.</blockquote><a href="/state/electorate-of-hesse">Electorate of Hesse</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 18th century the landgraves hired out troops to Britain, including some 17,000 Hessians for the American War of Independence, paying for works such as the Hercules monument and <b>Wilhelmshöhe</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/landgraviate-of-hesse-kassel">Landgraviate of Hesse-Kassel</a></li></ul></section>
